{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2173-08-07 03:11:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee, one of the hospitalists. You're here because of worsening abdominal pain near your G-tube site?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2173-08-07 03:12:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, it’s been getting worse since my last discharge. It’s deep around the tube and in my upper belly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2173-08-07 03:13:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Any nausea, vomiting, or fever?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2173-08-07 03:14:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Just some nausea. No vomiting, no fever."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2173-08-07 03:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay. I see you had a G-tube exchange in clinic yesterday and the pain got worse after that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2173-08-07 03:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Exactly. It spiked right after. I thought it might settle, but it hasn’t."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2173-08-07 03:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll check your labs and get a CT scan to make sure there’s no infection or other issue around the tube."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2173-08-07 03:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is it infected? The skin looks a little red."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2173-08-07 03:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "There’s some redness and a little drainage, but no spreading warmth or pus. Bariatric surgery saw you and thinks it might be superficial, but we’ll confirm with imaging."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2173-08-07 03:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the pain—could it be my ulcer again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2173-08-07 03:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s possible. You have a history of marginal ulcers, and that can cause deep epigastric pain. We’ll keep managing it while we investigate."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2173-08-07 04:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your labs came back—white blood cell count is normal, no sign of infection. Kidneys, liver, and glucose are stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2173-08-07 04:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2173-08-07 04:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. And your chest X-ray shows no free air, so no sign of a perforation."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2173-08-07 13:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "The CT scan is back. The G-tube is in the right place, balloon inflated in the stomach. There are some inflammatory changes in the muscle around the tube site, but no abscess or bleed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2173-08-07 13:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So no infection?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2173-08-07 13:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No deep infection. The inflammation might be from the recent tube manipulation. But since you’re not running a fever and your labs are clean, we don’t think you need antibiotics."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2173-08-07 13:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Then why does it still hurt so much?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2173-08-07 13:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It could be a combination—your known marginal ulcer, irritation from the tube changes, and possibly even the pain medicine itself. Sometimes long-term opioids can make pain more sensitive."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2173-08-07 13:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I didn’t know that could happen."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2173-08-07 13:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s called opioid-induced hyperalgesia. We’ll keep you on oxycodone for now but watch that closely. We also restarted your tube feeds to keep your blood sugar stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2173-08-07 13:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve been doing that at home, but I keep forgetting to check my sugar."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2173-08-07 13:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re checking it every six hours here. It’s been stable. You must keep the feeds running continuously to avoid hypoglycemia."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2173-08-08 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. How’s the pain today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2173-08-08 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A little better, maybe 6 out of 10 now. Still constant."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2173-08-08 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That’s progress. Your blood pressure was low this morning—88 over 74. You were asymptomatic, but we think it’s from the atenolol and maybe some dehydration from the pain."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2173-08-08 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "My BP is always low when I’m in pain. I usually stop the atenolol."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2173-08-08 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We gave you fluids and stopped the atenolol. We recommend not restarting it. Beta blockers can mask low blood sugar symptoms, which is risky for you."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2173-08-08 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, I didn’t know that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2173-08-08 08:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Better to manage your heart rate without hiding hypoglycemia signs. We’ll discuss alternatives with your PCP."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2173-08-08 14:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Your repeat labs show your white count dropped a bit—3.8—but it’s not concerning since you’re not sick. Hemoglobin is stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2173-08-08 14:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that from the pain or the meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2173-08-08 14:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Could be stress-related. We’ll monitor it. The important thing is you’re not bleeding or infected."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2173-08-09 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well. Pain is improving, vitals stable. We’re planning to discharge you today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2173-08-09 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great. What about my pain meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2173-08-09 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll go home with oxycodone 10 mg every 4 hours as needed—84 tablets for two weeks. Plus Tylenol for baseline. But you’ll need to follow up with pain management for refills."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2173-08-09 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my liquid oxycodone? I couldn’t find it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2173-08-09 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Crushing pills in the tube isn’t safe. We’ll help you locate the liquid form. It’s easier on your system and avoids tube clogs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2173-08-09 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I’ll try again."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2173-08-09 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Also, keep using the mupirocin on the tube site twice daily to prevent infection. And continue your Prevacid and Carafate for the ulcer."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2173-08-09 09:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And the EGD? Wasn’t I supposed to have that?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2173-08-09 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, still planned. Follow up with GI—they’ll schedule it. It’ll help us check the ulcer directly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2173-08-09 09:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my atenolol—I won’t restart it?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2173-08-09 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, we advise against it. Talk to your doctor about other options. Safety first with your hypoglycemia risk."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2173-08-09 09:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. Thank you for explaining everything."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2173-08-09 09:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. We’ll get your discharge papers ready. Follow up with your PCP in a week, and bariatric surgery if pain worsens."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2173-08-09 10:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just a final check—pain is at 5, you’re moving well, and your vitals are stable. All set for discharge."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2173-08-09 10:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel ready. Thanks for helping me through this."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2173-08-09 10:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well. Take care, and don’t hesitate to call if pain gets worse or you run into issues with meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2173-08-09 10:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Have a good day."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2173-08-07 03:11:00",
    "admission_end": "2173-08-09 14:21:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted with recurrent abdominal pain centered at the G-tube site and epigastric region, worsening after a recent G-tube exchange in clinic. Initial evaluation revealed no infection, obstruction, or perforation on imaging. CT showed ill-defined inflammatory changes around the G-tube insertion site without abscess. Pain was managed with oxycodone, and tube feeds were continued to prevent hypoglycemia. The patient developed asymptomatic hypotension, likely due to atenolol and pain-related hypovolemia, prompting discontinuation of atenolol to avoid masking hypoglycemia symptoms. Pain improved during the stay, and the patient was discharged home with a two-week supply of oxycodone, instructions to follow up with pain management and GI for EGD, and advised to discontinue atenolol permanently.",
    "problems": [
      "Abdominal pain, epigastric",
      "Chronic gastrojejunal ulcer without mention of hemorrhage or perforation, without mention of obstruction",
      "Hypotension, unspecified",
      "Gastrostomy status",
      "Other chronic pain",
      "Post-bypass hypoglycemia",
      "Marginal ulcer",
      "Depression",
      "Anxiety",
      "Insomnia",
      "Esophageal reflux"
    ]
  }
}
